FAILURE FILED (Zach-directed, 2026-07-29, from a /ideate groc-mangr session): THE FREEZE GATES DISPATCH, BUT IT IS NAMED AND UNDERSTOOD AS A GLOBAL STATE. Two of the three write paths into the ecosystem are outside it. crt's runaway loop is the first cost; this session is the second, and it happened while filing this.

Zach's framing, his words: "crt just ran in a runaway loop because nobody told it that [the ecosystem is temporarily frozen pending a move] ... part of this ecosystem is naming our failures. this one was ours." Ownership assigned to the ecosystem, not to crt. Correct Ashby posture: not who slipped, what regulator is missing at this interface.

RE-PROBED, NOT QUOTED. First draft of this filing was going to say "there is no global halt mechanism." That is FALSE and the re-probe caught it. schedule/FREEZE exists and is engaged (983ed3d 'FREEZE: ENGAGE', 6bed73f host-scope exemptions, file mtime 01:26). bin/freeze-check.sh is called at DISPATCH time by usage-paced-runner.sh per participant and by scheduler-run; it fails CLOSED (exit 1 frozen, exit 2 unreadable = also frozen); it supports per-project EXEMPT lines and currently exempts scheduler@dexter for the bootstrap play. The mechanism is well built. THE FAILURE HAPPENED ANYWAY, and that is what makes it worth filing.

THE THREE WRITE PATHS, AND WHICH ONE THE FREEZE ACTUALLY BINDS:

1. PACED DISPATCH -- regulated. freeze-check at dispatch, fails closed, picked up within ~5 min on both hosts. Works.

2. WORK ALREADY IN FLIGHT -- DECLARED gap, and it is the one that cost crt. schedule/FREEZE says so in its own text, verbatim: "work already in flight. This stops the NEXT dispatch, not a running job. dexter was mid-run on `crt` when this landed; that run completes." So the runaway was not unforeseen. It was written down, accepted, and then it happened. There is no in-flight cancellation path -- a freeze cannot reach a job that is already running, and a runaway loop is precisely the job that will not end on its own.

3. INTERACTIVE SESSIONS -- UNDECLARED gap, absent from the file's own NOT COVERED list, and the highest-variety path of the three. Exhibit, this session: started 01:38, twelve minutes AFTER the freeze engaged at 01:26, and pushed 9b174dd (scheduler) and e8da2c5 (groc-mangr) to two origins. Nothing consulted freeze-check on its behalf, because an interactive session does not dispatch. A human-invoked agent can write to every repo in the ecosystem; the freeze whose stated purpose is "ordinary paced dispatch must not run underneath the migration" does not see it. Zach engaged the freeze and then opened this session himself, which is the point -- the freeze lives in a file that agents read at dispatch and in a human's memory, and neither channel reaches an interactive session at session start.

THE DOCTRINE-SHAPED PART, and the reason to file rather than just fix:

A DECLARED GAP IS NOT A REGULATED ONE, AND DECLARING IT SPENDS ATTENTION EXACTLY ONCE. schedule/FREEZE is unusually honest -- it lists what it does not cover, at the point of refusal, every time it refuses. That honesty is real and should be kept. But a gap written into a file reads as HANDLED to every subsequent reader, including the human who wrote it. The in-flight gap was documented at 01:26 and cost a runaway loop the same night. This is the sibling of false-LIVE (realisateur .scheduler/FOCUS.md, 2026-07-27): false-LIVE is a green reading that is true of the configuration and false of the work; a declared-and-unregulated gap is a TRUE reading that has been converted into silence by having been written down. Both repel attention. Neither escalates.

AND THE FREEZE'S NAME IS DOING WORK ITS MECHANISM DOES NOT. The file says "participants are frozen while scheduler bootstraps itself onto dexter" and "ordinary paced dispatch must not run underneath the migration." The first sentence describes a global state; the second describes a dispatch gate. Only the second is implemented. Every reader -- human and agent -- takes the first. Naming a partial regulator after the total state it approximates is how a 2-of-3 gate gets used as a 3-of-3 guarantee.

MISSING REGULATOR (proposed, realisateur's to shape, not decided here):

- BIND THE FREEZE AT SESSION START, NOT ONLY AT DISPATCH. The cheapest correct form is a SessionStart hook reading freeze-check, so an interactive session in ANY repo is told at turn one. This is PLAYBOOK Play 1's third exhibit and its sharpest one: that play's finding is that the entire build discipline is prose and ZERO Claude Code hooks exist anywhere in the ecosystem. Every incident Play 1 cites is an agent reading prose and not following it. This incident is worse -- the agent never had the prose. A hook is deterministic local shell, zero API cost, offline, fails loud: it IS the offline-first doctrine, and the freeze is the case where prose cannot work in principle, because the session that needs to read it has not read anything yet.
- AN IN-FLIGHT KILL PATH, so a freeze can reach a running job and not only the next one. This is a real design question (a cooperative checkpoint the runner polls vs. an external kill), and it is scheduler engine work, not realisateur's -- flag it, do not design it here.
- STATE THE THIRD GAP IN schedule/FREEZE ITSELF, immediately and regardless of the above. The file's NOT COVERED list is its best feature and it is currently incomplete in the direction of its highest-variety path. One line, today, costs nothing.

WHAT THIS SESSION DID NOT DO: it did not touch schedule/FREEZE, crt, or any hook. Filing only. Routed through `scheduler -i realisateur` deliberately -- check-project-busy reports realisateur BUSY with a live interactive session since 01:03, and the front door's staleness-checked commit path is the documented safe route against exactly that, where a direct FOCUS.md cross-write is not.

CONNECTS TO THIS SESSION'S OTHER FILING (9b174dd, scheduler, QUESTIONS.md contract amendment). That amendment regulates one direction: an agent re-probes the premises underneath a human instruction. THIS failure is the exact mirror -- the human held a premise (the ecosystem is frozen; do not do ordinary work) that no agent could probe, because it was not in any channel an agent reads at start. The two halves are one regulator and should be shaped together: premises flow BOTH ways across the human interface, and only one direction currently has a rule.
